// DeadFunctionElimination — the reachability prune that decides WHICH functions reach the back end.
//
// A function nothing can reach is still register-allocated, framed, encoded and linked: the whole
// expensive half of the compiler runs on code that can never execute. This pass runs ONCE, at the
// target-neutral backend entry (`buildBackend`, immediately after the sugar gate), so x64, arm64 and
// wasm all inherit it rather than each having to remember to re-add it — the same argument that put
// `assertNoSugarOps` there.
//
// ⭐ **WHY IT IS A PREREQUISITE, not a nicety.** The compiler compiles `stdlib/*.maxon` source into every
// program. Without this pass, loading one stdlib module drags EVERY function in it into EVERY
// program — which adds them to all 221+
// committed goldens, costs every compile, and BREAKS the wasm and arm64 suites outright, because the
// GT/clock/IOCP substrate those modules reach is x64-windows-only (a `sleep(1)` program dies on wasm at
// `StdToWasm: emitFuncAddr: no wasm function index for '__gt_trampoline'`). Reachability fixes all
// three at once.
//
// ⭐ **WHO DECIDES WHAT, AND WHY THE TWO DECIDERS CANNOT DISAGREE.** `RuntimeUsage`
// (`StringRuntime.scanRuntimeUsage` + `noteDestructorUsage`/`noteCascadeUsage`) and this pass answer two
// DIFFERENT questions, in that order:
//
//   * `RuntimeUsage` owns INSTALLATION — *does this program carry the allocator / the GT scheduler /
//     the child-pipe host chunks / this `__destruct_<T>` at all?* It must run BEFORE lowering, because it
//     decides which bodies are built and which hand-assembled chunks and import bands the image carries.
//   * This pass owns REACHABILITY — *of what was installed, what can actually be reached from the
//     program's roots?* It runs LAST, on the finished Std module, and it never installs anything.
//
// So the composition is one-way and total: usage decides the FLOOR, DFE prunes within it, and DFE can
// only ever REMOVE. There is no question both answer, hence nothing for them to disagree about. That
// asymmetry is what makes an all-or-nothing install (`installManagedMemoryRuntime` installs the whole array
// runtime as one closure, because the helpers call each other) both correct and cheap: the closure is
// the safe over-approximation, and this pass takes the slack back.
//
// ⭐ **THE RUNTIME'S `.data` WORDS FOLLOW THIS PASS, NOT THE FLOOR.** `GlobalDataTable.layOut` runs after
// it, in `buildBackend`, and keeps a walked runtime word only if a SURVIVING function names it in a
// `globalAddr` — the label set this walk collects beside the reachability answer (`DeadFunctionSurvivors`).
// A word installed but never reached costs nothing, which the floor alone cannot say: a dead user function
// calling `__Builtins.schedMaxActiveWorkers()` keeps the query's body out of the image and its word with it.
// A word a hand-assembled chunk or the entry stub reads is `declared` by its producer instead, because no
// walk over Std ops sees that reader (`DataReach`).
//
// ⭐ **CONSERVATIVE BEATS CLEVER.** A missed prune costs bytes; a wrong prune is a link-time panic or,
// worse, a silently missing side effect. Every reference this pass cannot SEE is therefore a ROOT, and
// there are exactly three kinds of them — see `seedRoots`. A wrong prune does at least fail LOUD on
// every backend (`resolveCallFixups: call to unknown function`, `emitCall: no wasm function index`,
// `bakeFuncAbs64Relocs: … is in no debug symbol`), never silently.
//
// ⭐ **WHAT IT DOES NOT DO: it prunes FUNCTIONS, never `.rdata`.** Pruning witness/layout blobs whose
// label no live function loads would need a witness-conditional root sidetable, an init write-set map,
// a second "live vars excluding inits" pre-walk, and a hand-maintained allow-list of name prefixes.
// Keeping the data and pruning only code is what lets the root set below stay three lines long:
// because every `.rdata` function slot survives, every function a slot NAMES must survive too, and
// "is this label reachable?" never has to be asked. Dead-`.rdata` elimination is a
// separate change with its own goldens (`.rdata` offsets move, so `RequiredData` moves with them).
//
// ⚠ **A USER GLOBAL'S SLOT IS DECIDED UPSTREAM, AND DELIBERATELY NOT HERE**: a dead top-level global's slot
// is never BUILT, by `IR/Maxon/DeadGlobalElimination` filtering the arena in `compileToCodeResult`, and it
// reaches `layOut` as a `declared` entry. So the two never both answer for a user global, and nothing here
// needs a `shouldKeepGlobal` list; the label set this pass collects decides runtime words alone.
//
// Orphaned blocks and ops are LEFT BEHIND, exactly as every block-rebuilding pass leaves them
// (`IrModule.liveOpIndices`' header). `compactOps`/`compactBlocks` would reclaim them, but they cost
// an O(ops) walk plus two ops-sized arrays to release memory the module drops a few milliseconds
// later, at the end of the compile that allocated it. Nothing downstream walks the flat arrays.

// The `reached` column is DENSE over `0 .. funcCount-1`, so a name is hashed EXACTLY ONCE per
// call-graph edge — at the `FunctionNameIndex` lookup that resolves it to an index — and the worklist
// then carries scalars. A worklist of name STRINGS would re-hash one on every pop plus a `seen` insert
// per edge, three byte-sequence hashes per edge where this pays one.
//
// That index is also where a DUPLICATE NAME is refused, and this pass is the LAST site that builds one over
// the Std module — the only one downstream of every `install*Runtime`, hence the only one that can see a
// declaration colliding with an installed runtime symbol. The policy, and what each site can actually
// see, live in `FunctionNameIndex`.

// Drop every function unreachable from the program's roots, in place. Rebuilds `module.functions`;
// the dropped functions' blocks and ops stay in the flat arrays as orphans (see the header).
//
// ⭐ **IT RETURNS AN INDEX OVER THE ARRAY IT LEAVES BEHIND, AND THAT REPLACES A WALK RATHER THAN ADDING
// ONE.** Every backend derives at least one name-keyed map from the post-prune function array, and
// the pruning is what makes the index built above stale — positions move. So the fresh one is built HERE,
// billed to this phase, by the pass that invalidated the old one: a backend cannot then be handed a
// module without an index, and the index it is handed cannot describe a different array than the module
// it accompanies. The map it replaces is `buildCalleeResultClasses`, which walked the same array to store
// a value the index can already reach in two O(1) steps.
//
// ⭐ **AND THE `.data` LABELS THE SURVIVORS NAME COME BACK WITH IT**, collected by the same op walk that
// finds the call edges, so the layout that reads them cannot count a body this pass did not.
export function eliminateDeadFunctions(module StdModule, globalData GlobalDataTable, project Project) returns DeadFunctionSurvivors throws CompileError
	let index = try FunctionNameIndex.create(module.functions, project: project)
	let byName = index.byName

	var reached = BoolArray.create()
	reached.resize(module.funcCount())
	var worklist = FuncIndexArray.create()

	var reference = StdFunctionReference.create()
	var referencedGlobals = ReferencedGlobalLabelSet.create()
	try seedRoots(globalData, project: project, index: index, reached: reached, worklist: worklist)

	while worklist.count() > 0 'walk'
		let idx = try worklist.pop() otherwise panic("eliminateDeadFunctions: pop from a non-empty worklist")
		let func = module.getFunc(idx)
		requireNotSplicedAway(module, func: func, byName: byName, reached: reached)
		markReferencesOf(module, func: func, byName: byName, reached: reached, worklist: worklist, reference: reference, referencedGlobals: referencedGlobals)
	end 'walk'

	requireUnreachableLibraryStayedDead(project, byName: byName, reached: reached)
	sweepUnreachedFunctions(module, reached: reached, project: project)

	// The survivors are a SUBSET of an array this pass already refused a duplicate over, so this build
	// cannot report one — it is here for the POSITIONS, which the sweep renumbered. It goes through the one
	// door anyway rather than a subset-only shortcut: a second construction path is a second place the
	// check could be skipped, which is the shape this whole mechanism exists to remove.
	let funcNames = try FunctionNameIndex.create(module.functions, project: project)
	return DeadFunctionSurvivors.create(funcNames, referencedGlobals: referencedGlobals)
end 'eliminateDeadFunctions'

// What the prune leaves behind: the index over the surviving functions, and every `.data` label one of them
// names in a `globalAddr` — the set `GlobalDataTable.layOut` keeps a walked runtime word for.
export type DeadFunctionSurvivors
	export let funcNames as FunctionNameIndex
	export let referencedGlobals as ReferencedGlobalLabelSet

	static function create(funcNames FunctionNameIndex, referencedGlobals ReferencedGlobalLabelSet) returns DeadFunctionSurvivors
		return Self{funcNames: funcNames, referencedGlobals: referencedGlobals}
	end 'create'
end 'DeadFunctionSurvivors'

// ⭐ **WHAT THE ENTRY STUB CALLS BY NAME, DERIVED ONCE.** The four stub calls that exist only in some builds
// — the scheduler's `__gt_run_main`/`__gt_drain_at_exit`, the leak gate, the DebugStream bracket and the
// coverage dump — are decided here and nowhere else: the reachability roots, the inliner's called-once census
// and the three stub emitters (`augmentWithRuntime` for x64, `appendArm64RuntimeChunks`,
// `buildWasmCoreModule`) all read this record, so what the roots keep alive and what a stub calls cannot
// disagree. Built from the usage record in `compileToCodeResult` and held on the project, because the census
// runs mid-pipeline where no usage record is in reach.
export type EntryStubCalls
	export let runsMainOnAGreenThread as bool
	export let checksLeaks as bool
	export let bracketsDebugStream as bool
	export let dumpsCoverage as bool

	export static function none() returns EntryStubCalls
		return Self{runsMainOnAGreenThread: false, checksLeaks: false, bracketsDebugStream: false, dumpsCoverage: false}
	end 'none'

	export static function fromUsage(usage RuntimeUsage) returns EntryStubCalls
		return Self{runsMainOnAGreenThread: usage.usesGt, checksLeaks: usage.usesHeap, bracketsDebugStream: usage.usesDebugStream, dumpsCoverage: usage.usesCoverage}
	end 'fromUsage'
end 'EntryStubCalls'

// ⭐⭐ **THE PRE-ELIMINATION PASSES BET ON THIS PASS, AND THIS IS WHERE THE BET IS SETTLED.** Every pass
// that runs BEFORE the back end skips a function `LibraryFacts.unreachable` names — `lowerMaxonToStd` (its
// body, and therefore its `.rdata`), `insertRangeChecks` (its guards), `scanRuntimeUsage` (its runtime
// floor) and `checkCalls` (its target refusal) among them — every one on the premise that THIS pass drops it.
// The two derivations are independent: `LibraryFacts` walks the MAXON module from `main` alone, while this
// walk starts from a strictly LARGER root set (the entry, the stub-called runtime symbols, and every
// function an `.rdata` slot names) over the STD module. So the premise is a claim about two algorithms
// agreeing, not a tautology, and it is checked rather than argued.
//
// ⭐ **AND EVERY ONE OF THEM BETS TWICE**, because each also skips a `runtime/` body no root reaches
// (`LibraryFacts.unreachedRuntimeTier`, through `bodyIsSwept`) — built either way, but swept here. That set
// is widened once the allocator's gate is known (`withCompilerCalledTierUnreached`), so it also holds the
// tier files the compiler DECLARES it calls into for a program whose installer mints no such call: see the
// second loop.
//
// ⚠ **AND THE FAILURE IT CATCHES IS SILENT, WHICH IS WHY IT IS A PANIC AND NOT A COMMENT.** A function
// whose body `lowerMaxonToStd` skipped is EMPTY; if this walk kept it, the program would link cleanly and
// call a function that does nothing — a wrong answer with no diagnostic anywhere, the worst outcome this
// file's header names.
//
// ⚠⚠ **WHAT ARRIVES HERE IS A COMPILER BUG.** An author's declaration of one of the four names kind 2 of
// `seedRoots` roots is refused at that root with a POSITIONED E4015, so it never arrives as an unpositioned
// panic. What does arrive is an edge `StdlibSource`'s Maxon-tier walk fails to model: a DATA edge (a
// witness-table or layout-descriptor slot), or a call from a body synthesized after that walk
// (`__module_init`, a service's `__loop`) that the walk does not credit at the op making that body live.
//
// COST: O(unreached library functions), bounded by the library source LOADED and independent of the user's
// program — the sets are iterated, never `module.functions`, so a growing user program adds nothing.
function requireUnreachableLibraryStayedDead(project Project, byName FuncIndexByName, reached BoolArray)
	for name in project.libraryFacts.unreachable 'eachSkipped'
		if thisWalkReached(name, byName: byName, reached: reached) 'reachedAnyway'
			panic("requireUnreachableLibraryStayedDead: '{String.from(name)}' is in LibraryFacts.unreachable — so `lowerMaxonToStd` lowered no body for it, `insertRangeChecks` emitted no guards, and the PARSER may never have built its body at all (`Parser.skipUnreachedFunctionBody`, gated by the same walk through `growStdlibBodyDemand`) — yet this pass reached it from the root set and would keep it. The two reachability derivations disagree: this pass reached it along an edge the Maxon-tier walk in StdlibSource does not model — a DATA edge (a witness-table or layout-descriptor slot), or a call from a body synthesized after that walk (`__module_init`, a service's `__loop`) that the walk does not credit at the op making that body live. Keeping it would emit an EMPTY function the program calls")
		end 'reachedAnyway'
	end 'eachSkipped'

	// ⛔⛔ **THE TIER'S HALF OF THE BET, AND IT STAKES BOTH KINDS OF FAILURE.** A tier body is BUILT
	// whatever this set says, so for a name the WALK proved unreached nothing here is empty — what that name
	// promised is that `scanRuntimeUsage` could skip it, and that skip decides which runtime floor,
	// hand-assembled host chunks and import bands the image carries; kept by this walk, the body survives to
	// call into a floor nothing installed, a link failure with no diagnostic. For a name
	// `withCompilerCalledTierUnreached` added the stake is the first loop's instead — the lowering skipped
	// the body outright, so keeping it is an EMPTY function the program calls. Both are caught here.
	for name in project.libraryFacts.unreachedRuntimeTier 'eachUncredited'
		if thisWalkReached(name, byName: byName, reached: reached) 'reachedAnyway'
			panic("requireUnreachableLibraryStayedDead: '{String.from(name)}' is in LibraryFacts.unreachedRuntimeTier — so `lowerMaxonToStd` lowered no body for it, `insertRangeChecks` emitted no guards, and `scanRuntimeUsage` credited none of its calls — yet this pass reached it from the root set and would keep it. The two reachability derivations disagree: this pass reached the tier entry along an edge the Maxon-tier walk in StdlibSource does not model, or an installer minted a call into a compiler-called tier file that `LibraryFacts.withCompilerCalledTierUnreached` filed unreached. Keeping it would emit an EMPTY body calling into a runtime floor this image never installed")
		end 'reachedAnyway'
	end 'eachUncredited'
end 'requireUnreachableLibraryStayedDead'

// Whether THIS pass's walk kept a library function, for a name the module may not define at all — the
// same non-case `markLiveFunction` handles.
function thisWalkReached(name ByteArray, byName FuncIndexByName, reached BoolArray) returns bool
	if let idx = try byName.get(name) 'aModuleFunction'
		return try reached.get(idx) otherwise panic("thisWalkReached: reached.get OOB at function index {idx}")
	end 'aModuleFunction'

	return false
end 'thisWalkReached'

// ⭐⭐ **THE ASSUMPTION THE DUPLICATE-NAME REFUSAL RESTS ON: NOTHING CAN SUPPRESS ONE SIDE OF THE PAIR
// BEFORE THE COUNT SEES IT.** A detector that counts two functions is walked around by anything that removes
// one of them first, and there IS a pass that could remove a side — `StdlibSource.reachableMaxonFunctionNames`
// computes `LibraryFacts.unreachable`, which gates which runtime floor is installed (`scanRuntimeUsage` skips
// the body of every function whose NAME is in that set). If a `Builtins.maxon` declaration of `__foo` could
// land in `unreachable`, the runtime's own `__foo` could go uninstalled, exactly ONE `__foo` would reach the
// name index, and the compile would SUCCEED with every emitted call bound to the author's stub.
//
// ⚠⚠ **IT CAN, AND NEITHER OF THE TWO OBVIOUS BARS STANDS.** They would be: *(1)* E2051 — no
// LOADED stdlib module may declare a `__` name; *(2)* the E2051-exempt
// file is never stdlib SOURCE. Both fail on `stdlib/Builtins.maxon`, which IS loaded: it is the
// E2051-exempt file AND stdlib source, so
// its four `__*_fromString` declarations are ordinary members of the space `unreachable` is drawn from.
// What closes the hole is STRUCTURAL rather than a
// property of which files happen to be loaded:
//
//   1. **SUPPRESSION AND EMISSION ARE DECIDED FROM THE SAME BODIES.** The bad outcome needs an EMITTED
//      call to `__foo` whose installer was gated off, and a lowered runtime call is emitted only while
//      lowering a body. `lowerMaxonToStd` skips exactly the set `scanRuntimeUsage` skips, so no body can
//      emit a call whose installer was never offered that body's usage. ⚠ If the two DISAGREE — lowering
//      emitting `__mm_alloc`/`__str_eq` calls out of bodies the usage scan skipped — only this pass
//      dropping the function keeps the dangling call harmless.
//   2. **THE DECLARATION IS NEVER TAKEN OUT OF THE MODULE.** That skip empties a body; it does not drop the
//      function, and `lowerMaxonToStd` says so and says this is why. So both sides of a real pair still
//      reach the count below, which is the assumption this whole paragraph is about.
//   3. MEASURED: no name `Builtins.maxon` declares is a name the compiler INSTALLS.
//      `__int_fromString`/`__float_fromString`/`__bool_fromString`/`__byte_fromString` are declarations the
//      compiler CALLS, never emits — so there is no pair, and the whole suite is green with the
//      module loaded. If that ever ceases to be true the pair exists and E4015 fires, by (2).
//
// ⚠ **WHAT WOULD REOPEN IT** is (1) or (2), not which modules load: making that skip DROP a function rather
// than empty it, or letting any pass decide a runtime INSTALL from a body some other pass still lowers.

// ⭐ **THE ROOT SET — the three reference kinds no Std op in this module spells.** Everything else is an
// ordinary call-graph edge and is found by the walk.
//
// 1. **`main`** — the program's entry. The entry stub that calls it (`prependEntryStub`,
//    `prependLinuxEntryStub`, arm64's hand-assembled chunk, wasm's synthesized `$run`) is built AFTER
//    this pass, at the Target tier, so its call is invisible here.
//
// 2. **The functions an ENTRY STUB or a hand-assembled Target-tier CHUNK calls by name.** Both are built
//    after this pass — the stubs in `augmentWithRuntime`/`buildWasmCoreModule`, the chunks as raw bytes
//    with `CallFixup`s resolved at concat — so their edges likewise cannot be walked. They were found by
//    enumerating every `targetName:`/`callDirect` in the stub and chunk builders:
//      * `__module_init` and `__maxon_global_cleanup` — the managed-global initialization and release the
//        entry stub brackets `main` with (see `ModuleInit`). Nothing in the module calls
//        either, and they are what makes every managed global's `.data` slot non-null before `main` and
//        every managed global's record freed after it — so pruning one would turn a leak-gated spec into a
//        null-pointer store or an exit-101 leak.
//      * `__mm_leak_check` — both entry stubs' leak gate (`appendLeakGate`, `Arm64Runtime:103`). Nothing
//        in the module CALLS it, so it survives only by being rooted here. ⭐ Its absence is LOUD on
//        every backend, and that is a property worth keeping deliberately: each gate emits its call from
//        `RuntimeUsage.usesHeap` — where installation is decided — never from "is the function still
//        there?", so a pruned root leaves a call with no callee. MEASURED by deleting this line:
//        x64 `resolveCallFixups: call to unknown function '__mm_leak_check'` (X64Backend:1794), arm64
//        `arm64ResolveCallFixups: bl to unknown function` (Arm64Backend:1420), wasm the `usesHeap` panic
//        in `StdToWasm.appendLeakGate`. ⚠ Restore that property if you touch a gate: no committed spec
//        makes the leak gate FIRE, so a gate that decided from the module instead would let this root's
//        removal turn every exit-101 test silently green.
//      * `__gt_ready_locked` and `__sched_wake_or_spawn` — a readiness walk's ready of a parked waiter and
//        the wake it may owe. A program's Std callers of them may all be unreachable while a hand-assembled
//        chunk still names them, so they are reached from data alone. MEASURED for a predecessor by adding
//        the call without a root: `panic at X64Backend.maxon:2280: resolveCallFixups: call to unknown
//        function '__sched_wake_or_spawn'`, seventeen `subprocess-builtins` cases at once, exactly the shape
//        `__mm_leak_check`'s note above predicts.
//      * `__gt_drain_at_exit` — the entry stub's scheduler teardown (`BackendDispatch.prependEntryStub`),
//        in exactly `__mm_leak_check`'s position and for exactly its reason.
//    A chunk-called name is rooted on the target gate its chunk rides, and a stub-called one on the fact the
//    stub emits its call from (`EntryStubCalls`) — while the REFUSAL below reads every name whatever the
//    gate says (`compilerOwnedRoots`); a name absent from this module is simply not found, so a root costs
//    nothing where the runtime it belongs to was never installed.
//
// 3. **Every function named by an `.rdata` slot** (`GlobalDataTable.pendingRdataRelocs`) — a layout
//    descriptor's `destroyFunc@40`/`copyFunc@32` and every witness-table method slot. These are
//    reached through DATA, not code: `witnessCall` loads a `.rdata` table and jumps, and the element
//    drop walk `callIndirect`s a pointer baked into `.rdata` by `bakeFuncAbs64Relocs`. This is exactly
//    the precedent `noteDestructorUsage` already set — "referenced from data = live" — and because
//    this pass never prunes `.rdata` (see the header), rooting ALL of them is not conservatism, it is
//    the only correct answer: the slot survives, so its target must be there to fill it.
//
// ⭐⭐ **KIND 2 CARRIES A PROVENANCE PREMISE OF ITS OWN, AND IT IS THE SECOND DOOR ONTO THAT DEFECT.**
// Every one of those names is the COMPILER's, and rooting one asserts that whatever answers to it is the
// compiler's function. `main` asserts the opposite — it must be the author's — so the two kinds cannot go through one
// door. Leaving the `__` half unchecked is LOUD IN THE WRONG WAY: MEASURED, a
// `stdlib/Builtins.maxon` declaration of `__mm_leak_check` / `__module_init` / `__gt_enqueue` in a program
// that installs NO runtime under that name panics in `requireUnreachableLibraryStayedDead` above, with no
// file and no line — the author's mistake wearing a compiler bug's clothes. The pair case never reaches it
// because the name index refuses first; this case has no pair to count, so nothing else sees it.
//
// ⚠ **REFUSED WHETHER OR NOT THE COMPILER EMITS ITS OWN COPY HERE.** Not rooting a parsed hit would compile
// — the declaration is dead code and would simply be pruned — but then `__module_init` would be legal in a
// program with no managed global and E4015 in one with a managed global, i.e. the legality of a NAME decided
// by which runtime floor some other program carries. Refusing makes the rule what it says it is: the name is
// the compiler's. Same code, same rule, second detection — see E4015's registry entry.
//
// ⚠ Kind 3's names are NOT checked this way and must not be: an `.rdata` witness slot names `Type.method`, a
// PARSED method, so parsed-ness there is the ordinary case rather than the error.
function seedRoots(globalData GlobalDataTable, project Project, index FunctionNameIndex, reached BoolArray, worklist FuncIndexArray) throws CompileError
	markLiveFunction(project.entryFunctionName, byName: index.byName, reached: reached, worklist: worklist)
	seedTestRoots(index, reached: reached, worklist: worklist)

	for root in compilerOwnedRoots(project.target, stub: project.entryStubCalls) 'eachOwnedRoot'
		try markCompilerOwnedRoot(root, project: project, index: index, reached: reached, worklist: worklist)
	end 'eachOwnedRoot'

	for reloc in globalData.pendingRdataRelocs 'eachRdataSlot'
		markLiveFunction(reloc.funcName, byName: index.byName, reached: reached, worklist: worklist)
	end 'eachRdataSlot'
end 'seedRoots'

// ⭐ **KIND 2, AS ONE ROSTER** — every name an entry stub or a hand-assembled chunk calls, on `target`. Read
// here to root them and by the inliner to keep them out of the called-once rule (a body the stub still
// calls would be copied, not moved). Rooting a name the module does not define is a no-op, so a lane or a
// build that installs none of a family costs nothing here.
//
//   * `__module_init` / `__maxon_global_cleanup` — the managed-global initialization and release the entry
//     stub brackets `main` with.
//   * `__mm_leak_check` — both entry stubs' leak gate, emitted from `RuntimeUsage.usesHeap` where
//     installation is decided; a pruned root leaves a call with no callee, loudly, on every backend.
//   * `__gt_ready_locked` / `__sched_wake_or_spawn` — a readiness walk's two callees, named by a
//     hand-assembled chunk unconditionally.
//   * `__sched_syscall_regain` and the bracket pair — called only from the Target tier's syscall handoff,
//     on the lanes that build it (`SchedRuntime.targetBracketsBlockingSyscalls`, whose gate the install
//     shares); an ungated root here would be the shape in which the two halves drift apart silently.
//   * `__gt_preempt_safe` / `__gt_async_preempt_park` — asynchronous preemption's two Std halves, called
//     only by the monitor's hand-assembled sender and by the trampoline, on the lanes that stop a thread
//     where it stands (`SchedRuntime.targetStopsThreadsForPreemption`, whose gate the install shares).
//   * `__gt_drain_at_exit` / `__gt_run_main` — the entry stub's scheduler teardown and its call into a
//     green-thread program.
//   * `__gt_preempt` — the preemption's Std half, called only from the hand-assembled `__gt_guard_tripped`'s
//     preempt arm.
//   * `__ds_init` / `__ds_shutdown` and `__cov_dump` — the entry stub's debugstream and coverage brackets.
//
// ⭐⭐ **ONE ROSTER, AND THE GATE RIDES ON EACH ENTRY RATHER THAN FILTERING THE LIST.** The roster is the
// VOCABULARY — every name the stub or a chunk can ever call, which is what makes each one the compiler's —
// and `isCalledHere` is the per-build fact: a stub call is rooted on the stub's own facts
// (`EntryStubCalls`), a chunk call on the TARGET gate its chunk rides. The two readers split on that flag:
// a declaration under ANY name here is refused (the legality of a name must not turn on which runtime
// floor this program carries — E4015's ruling), and only a name called HERE is rooted or kept out of the
// called-once rule. A second, ungated list would be the same roster spelled twice.
module function compilerOwnedRoots(target Target, stub EntryStubCalls) returns CompilerOwnedRootArray
	var roots = CompilerOwnedRootArray.create()
	roots.push(CompilerOwnedRoot.create(ModuleInitName, isCalledHere: true))
	roots.push(CompilerOwnedRoot.create(GlobalCleanupName, isCalledHere: true))
	roots.push(CompilerOwnedRoot.create(MmLeakCheckName, isCalledHere: stub.checksLeaks))
	roots.push(CompilerOwnedRoot.create(GtReadyLockedName, isCalledHere: true))
	roots.push(CompilerOwnedRoot.create(SchedWakeOrSpawnName, isCalledHere: true))
	roots.push(CompilerOwnedRoot.create(SchedSyscallRegainName, isCalledHere: targetBracketsBlockingSyscalls(target)))
	roots.push(CompilerOwnedRoot.create(SchedEnterSyscallName, isCalledHere: targetCallsTheSyscallBracket(target)))
	roots.push(CompilerOwnedRoot.create(SchedExitSyscallName, isCalledHere: targetCallsTheSyscallBracket(target)))
	roots.push(CompilerOwnedRoot.create(PreemptSafeName, isCalledHere: targetStopsThreadsForPreemption(target)))
	roots.push(CompilerOwnedRoot.create(AsyncPreemptParkName, isCalledHere: targetStopsThreadsForPreemption(target)))
	roots.push(CompilerOwnedRoot.create(GtDrainAtExitName, isCalledHere: stub.runsMainOnAGreenThread))
	roots.push(CompilerOwnedRoot.create(GtRunMainName, isCalledHere: stub.runsMainOnAGreenThread))
	roots.push(CompilerOwnedRoot.create(GtPreemptName, isCalledHere: true))
	roots.push(CompilerOwnedRoot.create(DsInitName, isCalledHere: stub.bracketsDebugStream))
	roots.push(CompilerOwnedRoot.create(DsShutdownName, isCalledHere: stub.bracketsDebugStream))
	roots.push(CompilerOwnedRoot.create(CoverageDumpName, isCalledHere: stub.dumpsCoverage))
	return roots
end 'compilerOwnedRoots'

// One name the compiler owns, and whether this build's stub or chunks call it (`compilerOwnedRoots`).
export type CompilerOwnedRoot
	export let name as ByteArray
	export let isCalledHere as bool

	static function create(name ByteArray, isCalledHere bool) returns CompilerOwnedRoot
		return Self{name: name, isCalledHere: isCalledHere}
	end 'create'
end 'CompilerOwnedRoot'

export typealias CompilerOwnedRootArray = Array with CompilerOwnedRoot

// ⭐⭐ **A FOURTH KIND OF ROOT: EVERY `test` DECLARATION.** Nothing CALLS a test — that is what a test IS,
// a body the program reaches by no edge — so this walk would drop every one of them and the binary would
// hold no tests at all. `specs/test-declaration.md`'s `survives-dead-function-elimination` pins exactly
// that, through its minted golden: the exit code cannot see it, because `main` returns 0 whether the test
// survived or not.
//
// ⚠ **IT IS RECOGNISED BY ITS NAME, AND THE NAME IS UNFORGEABLE.** `__test_` is in the space
// `Parser.requireUnreservedName` refuses to any declared name (E2051), so no source can wear the prefix and
// there is no side channel here to keep in step with the parser — see `Project.isTestFunctionName`.
//
// ⚠ **IT IS NOT A `markCompilerOwnedRoot`, AND THE PROVENANCE PREMISE IS THE OPPOSITE ONE.** Those roots
// assert the name is the COMPILER's and refuse a parsed declaration of it. A test's body is the AUTHOR's;
// only its NAME is minted. So it goes through the plain `markLiveFunction`, exactly as `main` does — and a
// module that declares no test roots nothing here.
function seedTestRoots(index FunctionNameIndex, reached BoolArray, worklist FuncIndexArray)
	for func in index.functions 'eachFunction'
		if isTestFunctionName(func.name) 'aTest'
			markLiveFunction(func.name, byName: index.byName, reached: reached, worklist: worklist)
		end 'aTest'
	end 'eachFunction'
end 'seedTestRoots'

// Refuse a declaration that took one of the names the compiler owns, and root the name where this build
// calls it. The refusal reads the whole roster and the root reads the gate, so a name is the compiler's in
// every program and kept alive only in the ones that call it. The provenance test is the SAME one
// `refuseDuplicateFunctionName` makes and reads the same field for the same reason: a parsed function
// carries a `sourceFilePath` and a synthesized one cannot.
function markCompilerOwnedRoot(root CompilerOwnedRoot, project Project, index FunctionNameIndex, reached BoolArray, worklist FuncIndexArray) throws CompileError
	let func = try index.funcNamed(root.name) otherwise 'notAModuleFunction'
		return
	end 'notAModuleFunction'

	if not func.sourceFilePath.isEmpty() 'declared'
		reportPositioned(project, code: ErrorCode.declarationTakesCompilerOwnedName, message: "declaration of '{String.from(root.name)}' takes a name the compiler owns: it roots that name into every program's reachability set, for a function it emits itself. This program installs no runtime under it, so the declaration is what the root would keep — with an EMPTY body, since the passes that lower bodies were told the name was unreachable. Rename the declaration", line: func.nameLine, column: func.nameColumn, filePath: func.sourceFilePath)
		throw CompileError.compileError
	end 'declared'

	if root.isCalledHere 'calledByThisBuild'
		markLiveFunction(root.name, byName: index.byName, reached: reached, worklist: worklist)
	end 'calledByThisBuild'
end 'markCompilerOwnedRoot'

// Mark `name` reachable and queue its body for the walk. A name this module does not define is not an
// error and not a miss: it is a hand-assembled chunk (`__gt_context_switch`, `__gt_trampoline`,
// `__gt_morestack`) or a root whose runtime this program never installed. There
// is nothing to keep and nothing to drop, so it is simply not queued.
function markLiveFunction(name ByteArray, byName FuncIndexByName, reached BoolArray, worklist FuncIndexArray)
	let idx = try byName.get(name) otherwise 'notAModuleFunction'
		return
	end 'notAModuleFunction'

	markLiveFunctionIndex(idx, reached: reached, worklist: worklist)
end 'markLiveFunction'

function markLiveFunctionIndex(idx FuncIndex, reached BoolArray, worklist FuncIndexArray)
	let alreadyReached = try reached.get(idx) otherwise panic("eliminateDeadFunctions: reached.get OOB at function index {idx}")

	if alreadyReached 'already'
		return
	end 'already'

	try reached.set(idx, value: true) otherwise panic("eliminateDeadFunctions: reached.set OOB at function index {idx}")
	worklist.push(idx)
end 'markLiveFunctionIndex'

// ⛔ A body the inliner spliced into its one call site and emptied must be reached by NOTHING: laid out it
// is a zero-byte chunk. Reaching one means a reference the called-once census did not count — the census
// reads `stdFunctionReferenceOf` and the roots `CalledOnceCensus.markRoots` names, so the door is one of
// those two — and the panic names the reference that got through.
function requireNotSplicedAway(module StdModule, func IrFunction, byName FuncIndexByName, reached BoolArray)
	if not func.bodySplicedAway 'aBodyIsThere'
		return
	end 'aBodyIsThere'

	panic("eliminateDeadFunctions: '{String.from(func.name)}' was spliced into its one call site and emptied, yet it is still reached — {referenceKeepingAlive(module, name: func.name, byName: byName, reached: reached)}. The called-once census (`CalledOnceCensus.markRoots`, `stdFunctionReferenceOf`) must count that reference as referenced-elsewhere so the body stays a call")
end 'requireNotSplicedAway'

// For the panic above: the first reached function whose live ops name `name`, or the root set when no
// function does. Walks every reached function, on the failure path only.
function referenceKeepingAlive(module StdModule, name ByteArray, byName FuncIndexByName, reached BoolArray) returns String
	let funcIndexOfName = try byName.get(name) otherwise panic("referenceKeepingAlive: '{String.from(name)}' is a function of the module and so is in the name index")
	var reference = StdFunctionReference.create()

	for funcIndex in 0 upto module.funcCount() 'eachFunc'
		let isReached = try reached.get(funcIndex) otherwise panic("referenceKeepingAlive: reached.get OOB at function index {funcIndex}")

		if not isReached 'notWalked'
			continue
		end 'notWalked'

		let func = module.getFunc(funcIndex)

		for opIdx in module.liveOpIndices(func) 'eachOp'
			let op = try module.ops.get(opIdx) otherwise panic("referenceKeepingAlive: live op index {opIdx} not in module.ops")
			stdFunctionReferenceOf(op, byName: byName, into: reference)

			let namesIt = match reference.kind 'edge'
				none gives false
				directCall or
					otherReference gives reference.funcIndex == funcIndexOfName
			end 'edge'

			if namesIt 'keepsIt'
				return "'{String.from(func.name)}' still names it (a direct call, a `tryCall` or a `funcAddr`)"
			end 'keepsIt'
		end 'eachOp'
	end 'eachFunc'

	return "a root (`seedRoots`) names it and no reached function does"
end 'referenceKeepingAlive'

// Every static callee edge out of one live function, and every `.data` label it names. Walks LIVE ops
// (`IrModule.liveOpIndices`), never the flat `module.ops` array: that array is append-only and holds the
// ops every prior block-rebuilding pass replaced, so scanning it would resurrect callees — and keep runtime
// words alive — from IR that no longer exists.
function markReferencesOf(module StdModule, func IrFunction, byName FuncIndexByName, reached BoolArray, worklist FuncIndexArray, reference StdFunctionReference, referencedGlobals ReferencedGlobalLabelSet)
	for opIdx in module.liveOpIndices(func) 'eachOp'
		let op = try module.ops.get(opIdx) otherwise panic("eliminateDeadFunctions: live op index {opIdx} not in module.ops")
		markCalleeOf(op, byName: byName, reached: reached, worklist: worklist, reference: reference)

		if stdOpNamesAGlobalLabel(op) 'namesASlot'
			referencedGlobals.insert(stdOpGlobalLabel(op))
		end 'namesASlot'
	end 'eachOp'
end 'markReferencesOf'

// The static reachability edge one `StdOp` names, if any. Read here to walk the call graph and by the
// inliner's called-once census, which counts the direct calls and refuses a callee anything else names.
function markCalleeOf(op StdOp, byName FuncIndexByName, reached BoolArray, worklist FuncIndexArray, reference StdFunctionReference)
	stdFunctionReferenceOf(op, byName: byName, into: reference)

	match reference.kind 'edge'
		none then break 'edge'
		directCall or
			otherReference then markLiveFunctionIndex(reference.funcIndex, reached: reached, worklist: worklist)
	end 'edge'
end 'markCalleeOf'

// The kind of function reference one `StdOp` makes: a direct `call`, or a reference that is not one.
module enum StdFunctionReferenceKind
	none
	directCall

	// A `tryCall` or a `funcAddr`.
	otherReference
end 'StdFunctionReferenceKind'

// ⭐ **A KIND AND AN INDEX, NOT A NAME — IN A CALLER-OWNED RECORD FILLED IN PLACE.** Every reader resolves
// the name through the index and stops there, so the enumerator resolves it once and files the index: a
// payload union carrying the name boxes a heap record per op of the program, and a record aliasing the
// match binding is refused. The record is handed in rather than returned, for `StdDefinedValues`' reason;
// its index is meaningful only under a kind other than `none`. A name the index does not know — a runtime
// body installed after the pipeline, a hand-assembled chunk — is `none`: nothing to mark, nothing to count,
// exactly what every reader does with an unresolvable name.
module type StdFunctionReference
	export var kind as StdFunctionReferenceKind
	export var funcIndex as FuncIndex

	module static function create() returns StdFunctionReference
		return Self{kind: StdFunctionReferenceKind.none, funcIndex: 0}
	end 'create'

	function recordNone()
		self.kind = StdFunctionReferenceKind.none
	end 'recordNone'

	function resolve(name ByteArray, resolvedKind StdFunctionReferenceKind, byName FuncIndexByName)
		let index = try byName.get(name) otherwise 'notAModuleFunction'
			self.recordNone()
			return
		end 'notAModuleFunction'

		self.kind = resolvedKind
		self.funcIndex = index
	end 'resolve'
end 'StdFunctionReference'

// THREE variants carry a function NAME — `call`, `tryCall` and `funcAddr` — and every one of them is a
// static edge:
//
//   * `call` / `tryCall` — the direct call graph.
//   * `funcAddr` — a function's ADDRESS, and it is a STATIC edge even though the call that consumes it
//     is indirect. It is the whole of the compiler's first-class-function support: `let f = triple` lowers to
//     `funcAddr(__fnref_triple)`, whose thunk body then `call`s `triple`; an async spawn takes the
//     spawned function's bare address; an element destructor is stamped into a descriptor slot the same
//     way. ⚠ This arm is load-bearing: a `funcAddr` target is NOT already rooted by a `funcRef` that
//     produced its value — that is false for a directly-emitted one, and the destructor is then
//     pruned.
//
// `callIndirect` and `witnessCall` deliberately name NO callee: they consume a code pointer that some
// `funcAddr` (named above) or some `.rdata` slot (rooted in `seedRoots`) already produced, so crediting
// them would be crediting the same edge twice with no name to credit it to.
//
// The arms NAME every variant of the union, so a variant added later FAILS TO COMPILE here rather than
// being classified by omission. A new variant that carries a callee name must be added as its own arm.
module function stdFunctionReferenceOf(op StdOp, byName FuncIndexByName, into StdFunctionReference)
	into.recordNone()

	match op 'op'
		// arith + control + the call band up to `call`: pure computation, branches, `ret`, `param`.
		const or
			binOp or
			unaryOp or
			cmp or
			binOpImm or
			cmpImm or
			div or
			mod or
			condBranch or
			branch or
			ret or
			param then break 'op'
		call(_, callee, _) then into.resolve(callee, resolvedKind: StdFunctionReferenceKind.directCall, byName: byName)

		// `retVoid` … `rdataAddr`: returns, `.data`/`.rdata` ADDRESSES (labels, not functions), loads,
		// stores, and the two OS primitives that name no Maxon function.
		retVoid or
			globalAddr or
			loadIndirect or
			storeIndirect or
			osAllocPages or
			osWriteStdout or
			rdataAddr then break 'op'
		tryCall(_, _, callee, _) then into.resolve(callee, resolvedKind: StdFunctionReferenceKind.otherReference, byName: byName)

		// `errorReturn` reads two local virtuals; `osExit` is an OS primitive.
		errorReturn or
			osExit then break 'op'
		funcAddr(_, funcName) then into.resolve(funcName, resolvedKind: StdFunctionReferenceKind.otherReference, byName: byName)

		// `callIndirect`/`witnessCall` (see above) plus every appended `os*` primitive and `switch`,
		// none of which names a Maxon function.
		callIndirect or
			osPanic or
			osReadClock or
			osProcessSpawn or
			osProcessExitCode or
			osCloseHandle or
			osWaitHandle or
			osFreePages or
			switch or
			witnessCall or
			osPipeCreate or
			osEventCreate or
			osThreadCreate or
			osLockInit or
			osLockEnter or
			osLockLeave or
			osReadOverlapped or
			osLastError or
			osProcessSpawnInherit or
			osCancelIo or
			osGetPid or
			osPipeCreateOutbound or
			osWriteFile then break 'op'

		// The wall-clock read names no Maxon function either — its callee is a Win32 import slot.
		osReadWallClock then break 'op'

		// The THROWING witness call names no Maxon function, exactly as `witnessCall` does not: both read a
		// code pointer out of a `.rdata` witness table, and the DATA edge that keeps a witness impl live is
		// counted by the witness-table walk, not here.
		witnessTryCall then break 'op'

		// Every file op calls a Win32 IMPORT, not a Maxon function, so none keeps anything live here.
		osFileOpenRead or
			osFileClose or
			osReadFile or
			osFileOpenWrite or
			osFileSize or
			osFileDelete or
			osFileRename or
			osFileFindFirst or
			osFileFindClose or
			osFileFindNext or
			osDirCreate or
			osDirCurrentPath or
			osFileAttributes then break 'op'

		// The RENDERED panic reads a message pointer; in every other respect it is `osPanic`.
		osPanicValue then break 'op'
		osCommandLine then break 'op'

		// `GetStdHandle` and `GetModuleFileNameA` are Win32 IMPORTS, so neither keeps a Maxon function
		// live — the file band's reason, and the reason each is named separately from `osCommandLine`: an
		// op added later must fail to compile here rather than inherit one of their answers.
		osStdHandle then break 'op'
		osExecutablePath then break 'op'

		// The stderr write reaches the OS through an import or a syscall exactly as its stdout twin
		// does, so it keeps no Maxon function live either.
		osWriteStderr then break 'op'

		// A promoted record's address names a frame slot, not a function — it keeps nothing live.
		stackRecordAddr then break 'op'

		// O3: the three DebugStream host primitives call Win32 IMPORTS, so none keeps a Maxon function live.
		osOpenNamedSegment or
			osMapNamedSegment or
			osTickCountMs then break 'op'

		// The rest of the appended band — the four attached-subprocess host primitives, the `ws2_32` socket
		// band, the machine query AND the thread-CPU read — are import calls too (kernel32 and ws2_32), so none
		// keeps a Maxon function live either.
		osHandleSetInherit or
			osPipePeek or
			osEnvRead or
			osProcessTerminate or
			osSocketStartup or
			osSocketResolve or
			osSocketFreeAddrInfo or
			osSocketCreate or
			osSocketConnect or
			osSocketSend or
			osSocketError or
			osSocketRecv or
			osSocketClose or
			osCpuCount or
			osThreadCpuTicks then break 'op'

		// The decommit bottoms out in a Win32 import or a raw syscall — or, on wasm, in nothing at all —
		// so it keeps no Maxon function live either.
		osDecommitPages then break 'op'

		// A fill lowers to INLINE machine code on every target — a store ladder, never a call — so it
		// keeps no Maxon function live either.
		memFill then break 'op'

		// A reserve and a commit bottom out in `VirtualAlloc`, an `mmap` syscall or a `memory.grow` — never
		// in a Maxon function, so neither keeps one live.
		osCommitPages or
			osReservePages then break 'op'

		// The atomics and the `gs:` TLS read lower to INLINE machine code, and the thread token,
		// `TlsAlloc`, `TlsSetValue` and `SetEvent` to a raw syscall or an IMPORT — never to a Maxon function, so
		// no member of this range keeps one live. (Count the members off the range, not off this sentence.)
		atomicCas or
			atomicRmw or
			currentThreadToken or
			tlsAlloc or
			tlsSetValue or
			tlsSlotLoad or
			osSetEvent or
			osEnterBackgroundPriority then break 'op'
		osFileOpenWriteExecutable then break 'op'
		osEnvBlock or
			osEnvBlockFree then break 'op'

		// An ordered access is inline machine code on every lane, so it names no Maxon callee.
		loadAcquire or
			storeRelease then break 'op'
		osHandleFileType then break 'op'

		// The two named-section calls bottom out in kernel32 imports, never in a Maxon function.
		osCreateNamedSegment or
			osUnmapNamedSegment or
			osUnpublishNamedSegment then break 'op'

		// A coverage counter names a `.data` slot, not a callee.
		covPoint then break 'op'

		// A sampling, poller or socket op bottoms out in a host call, never in a Maxon function.
		osHostSample or
			osNetpoll or
			osSocketOverlapped or
			osSocketServe then break 'op'
	end 'op'
end 'stdFunctionReferenceOf'

// Rebuild `module.functions` keeping only what the walk reached.
//
// ⚠ **A REBUILD IS A RENUMBERING, and `module.functions` is not the only thing keyed by that numbering.**
// `Project.valueOrigins` (and the parameter spans inside it) file every row under a function's POSITION
// in this array, so dropping a function silently re-points every later row at its neighbour. The remap
// below is part of the sweep, not a courtesy after it — see `ValueOriginTable.remapFunctionIndices`.
function sweepUnreachedFunctions(module StdModule, reached BoolArray, project Project)
	let total = module.funcCount()
	var kept = IrFunctionArray.create()

	// Old index → new index, filled for EVERY old index so the remap can index it directly; the entries
	// for dropped functions are never read (the remap consults `reached` first).
	var newIndexOfOld = FuncIndexArray.create()

	// Did dropping renumber any function that SURVIVES? Only a survivor whose index moved has an origin
	// row to relocate; a drop with no survivor after it moves nothing (see the trailing-drop skip below).
	var keptNumberingShifted = false

	for i in 0 upto total 'eachFunc'
		let func = module.getFunc(i)
		let isLive = try reached.get(i) otherwise panic("eliminateDeadFunctions: reached.get OOB at function index {i}")
		let newIndex = kept.count()
		newIndexOfOld.push(newIndex)

		if isLive 'live'
			if newIndex != i 'moved'
				keptNumberingShifted = true
			end 'moved'

			kept.push(func)
		end 'live' else 'dead'
			// `String.from` runs INSIDE the lazy message, never beside it: this arm is taken once per
			// function the pass drops, so materializing the name eagerly would spend an allocation per
			// SUCCESS — the cost rising with exactly how well the prune works — to render a line that is
			// off on every build but a `--log=ir:trace` one.
			logTrace(LogCategory.ir, message: function() gives "dfe: dropping unreachable function '{String.from(func.name)}'")
		end 'dead'
	end 'eachFunc'

	let dropped = total - kept.count()
	logDebug(LogCategory.ir, message: function() gives "dfe: dropped {dropped} unreachable function(s) of {total}")
	module.functions = kept

	// Nothing dropped ⇒ `newIndexOfOld` is the identity and no row loses its function, so the remap could
	// only rebuild the origin columns into an exact copy of themselves. Skipped rather than absorbed into
	// `remapFunctionIndices`, because the saving is the whole REBUILD (the columns are sized by the
	// program's value count, so the copy is linear in program size): measured +487,704 … +20,528,128 bytes
	// across the scale ladder, all of it on a corpus that drops nothing.
	if dropped == 0 'numberingUnchanged'
		return
	end 'numberingUnchanged'

	// ⭐ TRAILING DROPS ARE FREE TOO. When every dropped function sits AFTER every survivor — no
	// survivor's index moved — the remap would keep each survivor's rows at their existing index and
	// merely discard the trailing dropped rows. But those rows are `lookup`ed by (funcIndex, valueId)
	// and a dropped function's index is now beyond `module.functions`, so no survivor's lookup can ever
	// match one: leaving them costs a few extra iterations on the cold E5001 path and nothing else,
	// where rebuilding the program-sized columns costs the same linear bytes the `dropped == 0` case
	// avoids. Compiling stdlib source into every program makes this the common case: a stdlib module the
	// program does not use (e.g. Clock in a program that reads no clock) merges after the user source and
	// drops as a block, so a program that installs no emitted runtime (the runtime functions append AFTER
	// it) drops ONLY trailing functions — without this skip every such compile would pay the full rebuild
	// that loading stdlib newly forced on it. A program that DOES install runtime drops the module from
	// the MIDDLE
	// (the runtime sits after it), a survivor's index moves, and the remap correctly runs.
	if not keptNumberingShifted 'trailingDropsOnly'
		return
	end 'trailingDropsOnly'

	project.valueOrigins.remapFunctionIndices(reached, newIndexOfOld: newIndexOfOld)
end 'sweepUnreachedFunctions'
